#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121024 :tutorial/content "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\,\\,=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1} & {◻}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {△}_{1} & {△}_{2} & … & {△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {◻}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1+0 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f26cf5f4-fd6d-5502-94d6-4b34bd2568ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3011 :tutorial/title "The Linearity of the Determinant"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121046 :tutorial/content "One way to simplify a determinant calculation is to create a row (or column) containing all zeros except for one entry. Then, we can use a Laplace expansion across that row (or column) to compute the determinant.\n\nLet's demonstrate this method on the matrix $A$ below.\n\n$$\nA=\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}2 & 1 & {\\color{black}1} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {\\color{black}3} \\\\ 8 & 14 & 17\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can make entries to the right of $a_{21}=1$ equal to zero using elementary *column* operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one column to another column in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 14 & 17\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}: &={C}_{2}+(-2){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}: &={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(21-10) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4a9a955-bf0a-5e47-973a-fa7d6e4191be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3240 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121056 :tutorial/content "There is something interesting about the bases of a vector space, summarized in the theorem below:\n\nIf the vector space $V$ has a basis with $n$ vectors, then any basis of $V$ has exactly $n$ vectors.\n\nThe number $n$ of vectors in any basis of $V$ is called the **dimension of the space** $V$ and is denoted $\\textrm{dim}(V).$\n\nFor example, consider the linear span below:\n$$\nH = \\textrm{Span}\\bigg\\{\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{v}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 3 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{v}_2}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 6 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{v}_3}\n\t\\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nWe know that, in order to find a basis of the span, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $3$rd columns. This tells us that\n$\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $H.$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, *any basis* of $H$ will also have *exactly* two vectors. So, we have $\\textrm{dim}(H)=2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6700cc12-77d9-59b2-8ee8-826575be0c12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3517 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121057 :tutorial/content "We can build some geometric intuition for finite-dimensional vector spaces. To illustrate, let's find the dimensions of $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\},$ $\\mathbb{R},$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nFirst, note that $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ and $\\mathbb{R}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^1.$ We have the following standard bases:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{R}^0:\n\t\\big\\{\n\t\t\\textrm{no basis}\n\t\\big\\},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbb{R}^1:\n\t\\big\\{\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{i}}\n\t\\big\\},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbb{R}^2:\n\t\\Bigg\\{\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{i}}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{j}}\n\t\\Bigg\\},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbb{R}^3:\n\t\\Bigg\\{\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{i}}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{j}}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{k}}\n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, we have the following dimensions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}({R}^{0}) &=0,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{1})=1,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{2})=2,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{3})=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's build some geometric intuition. We can represent the standard bases in the following diagram:\n\n![](images/16/169b5597d89fa32fd09772593cc2a9a330ee12881281dea4e3e43dc6165b9ebd.png)\n\nSo, the sequence $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ $\\mathbb{R}^1,$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ $\\ldots$ can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/37/379823eca26d43337744a8f4a3872e67ac537a6c193d55e39b5d6761f258f6f5.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^0$ is represented geometrically as a $0$-dimensional point.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^1$ is represented geometrically as a $1$-dimensional line segment.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is represented geometrically as a $2$-dimensional square.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is represented geometrically as a $3$-dimensional cube.\n\nUnfortunately, we can't visualize dimensions greater than $3,$ but we know that $\\mathbb{R}^n$ has the following standard basis consisting of $n$ vectors:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\cdots, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n) = n.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "88c2cca4-9e7b-5ace-b239-798bcab0d26a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3518 :tutorial/title "Geometric Intuition for Finite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121069 :tutorial/content "As defined earlier, the dimension of a vector space $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$.\n\nHowever, note that the definition above is only applicable if a vector space has at least one basis with a finite number of elements.\n\n- If a vector space has a basis with a finite number of vectors, then it is called **finite-dimensional**.\n\n- Otherwise, if there is no finite basis, the vector space is called **infinite-dimensional**.\n\nIn linear algebra, we mostly consider finite-dimensional vector spaces." :tutorial/id #uuid "e433abf5-e941-5e63-9642-c3f90b4716a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4226 :tutorial/title "Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184159 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184063} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc8dc1a1-9606-5b67-942e-219ab2ddfc1c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050446}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186644115}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Power of a Nilpotent Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184160 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184064} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b51e8ff1-247b-5efd-a684-8fca6c064e1e" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186644105}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Power of an Idempotent Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184070} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049009}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673369} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673362}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184071} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673453} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673452}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184124} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcbc10c0-4e6b-5dac-ad87-f0175e4a53d2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673405} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673403}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185315 :tutorial/content "A matrix is said to be **nilpotent** if some positive power of the matrix is the zero matrix. For example, the matrix\n\n$$B = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nis nilpotent because its second power is the zero matrix:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =O\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTo state the definition more precisely: a matrix with the property $B^k=O$ for a number $k>0$ is known as a **nilpotent** matrix, and the lowest number $k$ such that $B^k=O$ is called the **index.**\n\nTherefore, our matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "39a61f3b-3f47-5962-a20c-33867d6f1127" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3130 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185316 :tutorial/content "A matrix is said to be **idempotent**, when multiplied by itself, yields itself.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$B = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nis idempotent because $B^2 = B$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot (-6)+(-6)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =B\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3977ba53-51f9-53db-b487-753096fe77e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3131 :tutorial/title "Idempotent Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185320 :tutorial/content "When we reduce a matrix to row echelon form, the number of *pivot* columns plus the number of *non-pivot* columns always equals the total number of columns of the matrix. \n\nBy translating this into the language of vector spaces, we get the **rank-nullity theorem**. The rank-nullity theorem relates the rank of a matrix to the dimension of its null space:\n\n*Let $A$ be a matrix (not necessarily square) with $n$ columns. Then,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A)) + \\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n),\n$$\n\n*or, equivalently,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that for some matrix $A,$ we have\n\n\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)= \\left\\{ \\left. \\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\:\\right|\\: \\, x_2,x_3\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n\n\nWe can use the rank-nullity theorem to calculate $\\textrm{rank}(A).$\n\nIn this case, we must have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, we must have $\\textrm{nullity}(A)=2,$ since \n\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)= \\left\\{ \\left. x_2 \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + x_3 \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\:\\right|\\: \\, x_2,x_3\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, using the rank-nullity theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0728c510-110b-5bde-93ea-9050a87a6ecc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3524 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
